                <pb facs="0572" n="560" sameAs="frus:frus1940v03/pg560"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/6661: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Athens</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-11-12T17:00:00+02:00">November 12,
                            1940—5 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 14—9:32 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>299. Department’s telegram No. 248, November 7, 6 p.m. In reply to my formal
                    inquiry the Under Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs informs me by his note
                    No. 33953 dated November 11, 1940, that “a state of war exists in Greece since
                    October 28th of this year at 5:30 a.m. at which time Italy launched an
                    unexpected attack against Greece”.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
